WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.630 --> 00:00:03.980
RICHARD HAASS: Well, good evening.

00:00:03.980 --> 00:00:06.420
Welcome to the Council on Foreign Relations.

00:00:06.420 --> 00:00:11.870
I'm Richard Haass, the president of this organization,
and I want to thank all of you, whether here

00:00:11.870 --> 00:00:17.510
in person or watching us through one or another
technology, for joining for us for tonight's

00:00:17.510 --> 00:00:22.099
Sorensen Distinguished Lecture on the United
Nations.

00:00:22.099 --> 00:00:28.981
Now, this is the first Sorensen Lecture since
Ted passed away in 2010, so what I'd like

00:00:28.981 --> 00:00:36.050
to do for a minute is recognize some of his
contributions to this institution and to this

00:00:36.050 --> 00:00:37.050
country.

00:00:37.050 --> 00:00:44.530
He would often joke -- about many things -- (laughter)
-- but one of the things Ted would joke about

00:00:44.530 --> 00:00:49.630
would -- is -- is on his epitaph, his name
would be misspelled -- (laughter) -- and that

00:00:49.630 --> 00:00:53.350
it would simply say something to the effect
that he had been President John F. Kennedy's

00:00:53.350 --> 00:00:56.970
speechwriter or alter ego or something along
those lines.

00:00:56.970 --> 00:01:04.900
And to be fair, historians could perhaps be
forgiven for focusing on those years, because

00:01:04.900 --> 00:01:09.689
Ted played such an important part of history
that was really critical not just for this

00:01:09.689 --> 00:01:12.179
country but for the world.

00:01:12.179 --> 00:01:16.939
But that said, we here at the Council on Foreign
Relations and beyond know that there was more

00:01:16.939 --> 00:01:20.060
to his career and much more to him than that.

00:01:20.060 --> 00:01:24.490
In his many speaking appearances here at the
Council on Foreign Relations over the decade

00:01:24.490 --> 00:01:32.659
covered not just issues like the Cold War
but also topics as varied as trade with Africa,

00:01:32.659 --> 00:01:38.119
peace in the Middle East, and my personal
favorite, conflict resolution and political

00:01:38.119 --> 00:01:39.750
reform in Tajikistan.

00:01:39.750 --> 00:01:40.750
(Laughter.)

00:01:40.750 --> 00:01:44.110
It was a veritable barn burner.

00:01:44.110 --> 00:01:50.719
I want to thank his wife, whose here with
us tonight, Gillian, for her part in the establishment

00:01:50.719 --> 00:01:55.770
of this -- of this lecture series, and more
broadly, being so generous with Ted's time

00:01:55.770 --> 00:01:57.229
over the years.

00:01:57.229 --> 00:02:00.840
We must have seemed like competition, I expect,
every now and then.

00:02:00.840 --> 00:02:05.770
Ted was good enough to serve on the board
of this organization and was really a central

00:02:05.770 --> 00:02:07.579
part of our intellectual community.

00:02:07.579 --> 00:02:12.780
He was also someone, and perhaps the only
person I know, who had the creativity and

00:02:12.780 --> 00:02:18.000
the courage to describe Pete Peterson, who
at that point was the chairman of the board

00:02:18.000 --> 00:02:25.870
of directors, as a self-made man who worships
his own creator.

00:02:25.870 --> 00:02:30.300
(Laughter.)

00:02:30.300 --> 00:02:34.140
I know that Ted would have been delighted
to welcome tonight's speaker.

00:02:34.140 --> 00:02:40.830
Ban Ki-moon is the eighth secretary-general
of the United Nations, and he's led that institution

00:02:40.830 --> 00:02:46.250
-- just several blocks to the east, in case
you were wondering -- since 2007, and he's

00:02:46.250 --> 00:02:52.050
worked hard, to say the least, to highlight
the importance of international action on

00:02:52.050 --> 00:02:59.020
challenges ranging from climate change to
food security to poverty to conflict resolution.

00:02:59.020 --> 00:03:03.840
And he's done this at a time, I would say,
of unusual challenge for this organization

00:03:03.840 --> 00:03:04.840
and for the world.

00:03:04.840 --> 00:03:10.500
It's all taken place against the backdrop
of global economic difficulty.

00:03:10.500 --> 00:03:17.480
We've also seen the truly historic upheavals
throughout the Middle East, including but

00:03:17.480 --> 00:03:24.000
not limited to revolutionary change in Egypt
and several other countries, a terrible civil

00:03:24.000 --> 00:03:30.510
war in Syria, and Iran's steady march toward
developing the capacity to produce nuclear

00:03:30.510 --> 00:03:31.660
weapons.

00:03:31.660 --> 00:03:35.840
And then beyond the Middle East, a lot else
has been going on, from the movement, if you

00:03:35.840 --> 00:03:40.710
will, of historical tectonic plates in Asia
and the Asia-Pacific region, which pose new

00:03:40.710 --> 00:03:44.870
challenges to regional and potentially global
stability.

00:03:44.870 --> 00:03:48.650
And then there's been the structural issue
at the same time of integrating many of the

00:03:48.650 --> 00:03:54.810
world's new powers, from China to India to
Brazil and others into the institutions of

00:03:54.810 --> 00:04:00.610
global governance, including the U.N., that
deal with issues ranging from climate change

00:04:00.610 --> 00:04:03.730
to trafficking in drugs and persons.

00:04:03.730 --> 00:04:09.550
In short, he has one of the world's most demanding
and most important inboxes.

00:04:09.550 --> 00:04:13.460
What we are going to do tonight -- and we
are fortunate indeed -- is to hear the secretary-general

00:04:13.460 --> 00:04:15.340
give -- make some opening remarks.

00:04:15.340 --> 00:04:21.750
Then he will show considerable bravery of
his own by being joined onstage by Christiane

00:04:21.750 --> 00:04:28.340
Amanpour, one of the country's and the world's
premiere journalists associated with both

00:04:28.340 --> 00:04:30.210
CNN and ABC.

00:04:30.210 --> 00:04:36.250
And they will have a short conversation, and
then he has agreed to open himself up to the

00:04:36.250 --> 00:04:38.870
questions of our membership who are with us
tonight.

00:04:38.870 --> 00:04:42.970
So ladies and gentlemen, please join me in
welcoming the secretary-general of the United

00:04:42.970 --> 00:04:45.180
Nations.

00:04:45.180 --> 00:04:47.400
(Applause.)

00:04:47.400 --> 00:05:05.870
SECRETARY-GENERAL BAN KI-MOON: Thank you,
Dr. Richard Haass, president of the Council

00:05:05.870 --> 00:05:14.560
on Foreign Relations, my good friend, Gillian
Sorensen, Ms. Christiane Amanpour of CNN,

00:05:14.560 --> 00:05:18.510
distinguished guests, ladies and gentlemen.

00:05:18.510 --> 00:05:25.880
It's a great pleasure to be here with you
today to share some of our thoughts about

00:05:25.880 --> 00:05:35.370
the current international world, and I am
very much moved by your warm welcome.

00:05:35.370 --> 00:05:44.650
Standing in this Council on Foreign Relations
today -- when it comes to timing of my speech

00:05:44.650 --> 00:05:51.960
today, I think my appearance tonight seems
to be sandwiched by two big events.

00:05:51.960 --> 00:05:59.001
Yesterday there was the Grammy awards -- (laughter)
-- tomorrow, most importantly, the President

00:05:59.001 --> 00:06:04.710
Obama is going to have his union address -- State
of the Union address.

00:06:04.710 --> 00:06:06.380
So I am sandwiched.

00:06:06.380 --> 00:06:11.650
Of course, you know, I know I should not try
to compete with President Obama.

00:06:11.650 --> 00:06:16.460
I don't have any such intention or ambition.

00:06:16.460 --> 00:06:24.820
Perhaps I am luckier than President Obama,
at least in one sense: this -- I am not belonging

00:06:24.820 --> 00:06:28.450
to either Democrats or Republican.

00:06:28.450 --> 00:06:33.980
You can feel free to applaud together in this
hall -- (laughter) -- whatever I may say,

00:06:33.980 --> 00:06:35.199
if you agree.

00:06:35.199 --> 00:06:42.930
And as for the Grammy awards, I know you are
broadcasting this event this evening, but

00:06:42.930 --> 00:06:47.010
I will not try to be a South Korean YouTube
sensation.

00:06:47.010 --> 00:06:48.010
(Laughter.)

00:06:48.010 --> 00:06:52.340
I promise you that no "Gangnam Style" for
me today.

00:06:52.340 --> 00:06:53.340
(Laughter.)

00:06:53.340 --> 00:06:55.350
In any event, thank you very much, again.

00:06:55.350 --> 00:07:02.250
I am especially honored to deliver this Sorensen
lecture on the United Nations.

00:07:02.250 --> 00:07:09.300
The Sorensen commitment to the principles
and goals of the United Nations is unrivaled.

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:19.180
Ted Sorensen wore U.N. blue helmet since the
model UNESCO exercise he took part in as a

00:07:19.180 --> 00:07:20.180
student.

00:07:20.180 --> 00:07:25.760
His law practice found many ways to advance
U.N. causes.

00:07:25.760 --> 00:07:33.500
Gillian Sorensen, your voice has reached across
this country and around the world.

00:07:33.500 --> 00:07:42.750
For decades now you have been a great U.N.
advocate, explaining what we do and why it

00:07:42.750 --> 00:07:44.020
matters.

00:07:44.020 --> 00:07:52.490
I know how much it pains you and Ted when
the United Nations was marginalized or misunderstood

00:07:52.490 --> 00:07:55.790
-- not much appreciated.

00:07:55.790 --> 00:08:02.280
This annual lecture has helped to serve as
a welcome corrective.

00:08:02.280 --> 00:08:08.880
You may all be aware that my own career was
started in diplomacy when I was a -- as a

00:08:08.880 --> 00:08:14.680
young high school student when I was lucky
enough to have met -- to meet the president,

00:08:14.680 --> 00:08:17.630
John F. Kennedy, in 1962.

00:08:17.630 --> 00:08:27.580
One minute at the time, I was just a very
poor rural boy visiting from war-torn South

00:08:27.580 --> 00:08:28.580
Korea.

00:08:28.580 --> 00:08:33.690
The next, standing there in the Rose Garden
in 1962.

00:08:33.690 --> 00:08:42.849
I was determined and inspired to do my own
role to do for my country and leader, if possible,

00:08:42.849 --> 00:08:44.980
to work for the world.

00:08:44.980 --> 00:08:49.420
That is what I'm here.

00:08:49.420 --> 00:08:55.250
I have wondered about Ted Sorensen's whereabouts
at the time.

00:08:55.250 --> 00:09:03.850
Was he either watching us or more likely -- most
likely he must have been bent in on a typewriter,

00:09:03.850 --> 00:09:07.890
wrestling another historic, memorable phrase
into being.

00:09:07.890 --> 00:09:15.420
And, anyway, whatever the case may be, I feel
fortunate that my life has been touched by

00:09:15.420 --> 00:09:18.960
the Sorensens' spirit of service.

00:09:18.960 --> 00:09:24.070
Ladies and gentlemen, you all know how many
issues are on the United Nations' agenda.

00:09:24.070 --> 00:09:31.370
I cannot possibly cover all of the issues
which is now being addressed by the United

00:09:31.370 --> 00:09:43.700
Nations by myself, and by very, very dedicated
U.N. staff in this very limited time.

00:09:43.700 --> 00:09:49.410
Therefore, I have done what is typically a
very dangerous thing for a U.N. secretary-general

00:09:49.410 --> 00:09:50.410
to do.

00:09:50.410 --> 00:09:57.940
I have chosen just two items above all others
to discuss in depth.

00:09:57.940 --> 00:10:03.890
These two items both have huge consequences
for humanity.

00:10:03.890 --> 00:10:10.380
In both cases, the international community
is not upholding its responsibility.

00:10:10.380 --> 00:10:22.390
Both risk harsh judgment of history should
current trends continue, and both require

00:10:22.390 --> 00:10:26.690
collective action that must involve the United
Nations.

00:10:26.690 --> 00:10:29.270
What are those two issues?

00:10:29.270 --> 00:10:36.890
I speak about the crisis in Syria and the
threat of climate change.

00:10:36.890 --> 00:10:43.700
We can use the question-and-answer session
to cover other issues, like situations in

00:10:43.700 --> 00:10:49.250
Mali, Afghanistan, Democratic Republic of
Congo or Democratic People's Republic of Korea

00:10:49.250 --> 00:10:51.460
-- just a few.

00:10:51.460 --> 00:10:58.440
But for me at this time, Syria and climate
deserve our heightened attention.

00:10:58.440 --> 00:11:03.480
Ladies and gentlemen, Syria is self-destructing.

00:11:03.480 --> 00:11:13.540
After nearly two years, we no longer count
-- we no longer count days in hours but in

00:11:13.540 --> 00:11:14.540
bodies.

00:11:14.540 --> 00:11:22.620
Another day, another hundred, 200 or 300 are
dead.

00:11:22.620 --> 00:11:27.440
Fighting rages; sectarian hatred is on the
rise.

00:11:27.440 --> 00:11:31.990
The catastrophe -- the catalogue of war crimes
is mounting.

00:11:31.990 --> 00:11:34.800
Sexual violence is widespread.

00:11:34.800 --> 00:11:39.710
The destruction is systematic.

00:11:39.710 --> 00:11:44.810
Syria is being torn apart limb by limb.

00:11:44.810 --> 00:11:49.820
The political crisis has spread a humanitarian
emergency.

00:11:49.820 --> 00:11:57.590
Four million people -- that's 20 percent of
total population of Syria -- 1-out-of-5 are

00:11:57.590 --> 00:12:02.750
in need of immediate humanitarian assistance.

00:12:02.750 --> 00:12:09.780
Three-quarters of a million have fled the
country, straining Syria's neighbors and further

00:12:09.780 --> 00:12:13.500
risking regional instability.

00:12:13.500 --> 00:12:19.460
The pledges from the donor conference two
weeks ago, which I convened in Kuwait, will

00:12:19.460 --> 00:12:22.760
help us to bring comfort to those in need.

00:12:22.760 --> 00:12:29.740
But it can never be enough, however, to treat
the symptoms of conflict.

00:12:29.740 --> 00:12:38.589
Our responsibility is to get to the roots
and make the violence stop before the moment

00:12:38.589 --> 00:12:48.589
divides rules the day inside Syria, in the
region and the United Nations Security Council.

00:12:48.589 --> 00:12:52.760
The Syrians are not ready to talk to one another.

00:12:52.760 --> 00:12:58.209
The regime remains as repressive as ever.

00:12:58.209 --> 00:13:03.490
The situation cries out for action by the
Security Council, in particular.

00:13:03.490 --> 00:13:11.769
As Joint Special Representative Dr. Brahimi
has said, the council cannot simply wait for

00:13:11.769 --> 00:13:15.630
divisions to evaporate.

00:13:15.630 --> 00:13:22.750
That guarantees not only more violence, but
the very disintegration of the country and

00:13:22.750 --> 00:13:29.430
the possibility that Syria could become an
arena for competing national and regional

00:13:29.430 --> 00:13:35.180
forces, government and non-state actors alike.

00:13:35.180 --> 00:13:42.970
The Security Council must no longer stand
on the sidelines, deadlocked, silently witnessing

00:13:42.970 --> 00:13:44.600
the slaughter.

00:13:44.600 --> 00:13:53.420
It must be willing, at long last, to come
together and establish the parameters for

00:13:53.420 --> 00:13:57.870
the democratic transition that can save Syria.

00:13:57.870 --> 00:14:06.649
The offer by national coalition leader, Moaz
al-Khatib to open discussions with representatives

00:14:06.649 --> 00:14:15.770
of the Syrian government is an opportunity
they should not miss -- a chance to switch

00:14:15.770 --> 00:14:22.899
from a devastating military logic to a promising
political approach.

00:14:22.899 --> 00:14:26.720
This was a courageous offer by Mr. al-Khatib.

00:14:26.720 --> 00:14:34.610
I urge both Syrian government and the Security
Council to respond positively.

00:14:34.610 --> 00:14:41.300
We need to find a way towards negotiations
between empowered government and opposition

00:14:41.300 --> 00:14:49.890
delegations that can made key decisions about
the country's future -- on elections, on constitutional

00:14:49.890 --> 00:14:58.310
reform, on accountability for victims of international
crimes, on other steps needed to meet the

00:14:58.310 --> 00:15:03.370
people's legitimate aspirations.

00:15:03.370 --> 00:15:10.940
It is time for a clean break from Syria's
past and for a decisive turn toward a future

00:15:10.940 --> 00:15:19.890
where Syrians are able to express their political
views freely, without fear of arbitrary arrest

00:15:19.890 --> 00:15:26.010
or killing -- a Syria where human rights of
all are protected.

00:15:26.010 --> 00:15:31.400
Let us also draw a broader lesson.

00:15:31.400 --> 00:15:40.890
In the Arab world and elsewhere, people want
real change, not grudging, cosmetic adjustment.

00:15:40.890 --> 00:15:48.620
Let us remember that whether countries are
emerging from conflict or authoritarian rule,

00:15:48.620 --> 00:15:59.519
transition will not be linear marches toward
Jeffersonian democracy or Swiss-style tranquility.

00:15:59.519 --> 00:16:02.779
The road will be rocky.

00:16:02.779 --> 00:16:11.040
The cases of Myanmar, Timor-Leste and Sierra
Leon give some hope that patient, long-term

00:16:11.040 --> 00:16:15.860
engagement, consistent diplomacy can yield
results.

00:16:15.860 --> 00:16:25.050
The case of Mali shows how little we sometimes
understand about the fragility of democracies

00:16:25.050 --> 00:16:28.959
considered well- established.

00:16:28.959 --> 00:16:36.089
We must also resist the temptation to see
the current troubles in Egypt or Libya as

00:16:36.089 --> 00:16:41.790
proof that the old order was a better one.

00:16:41.790 --> 00:16:49.720
We can take some confidence from history that
period of -- periods of post- revolutionary

00:16:49.720 --> 00:16:52.470
disorder are passing phenomena.

00:16:52.470 --> 00:16:58.170
But close and patient engagement will be crucial.

00:16:58.170 --> 00:17:04.860
The international community has a duty to
accompany these transitions with meaningful

00:17:04.860 --> 00:17:06.309
contributions.

00:17:06.309 --> 00:17:15.490
Ladies and gentlemen, let me now turn to the
gathering threat of climate change.

00:17:15.490 --> 00:17:19.400
Scientists have long sounded the alarm.

00:17:19.400 --> 00:17:26.049
Top-ranking military commanders and security
experts have now joined the chorus.

00:17:26.049 --> 00:17:32.610
Yet, the political class seems far behind.

00:17:32.610 --> 00:17:40.419
You all know the potential consequences: the
downward global spiral of extreme weather

00:17:40.419 --> 00:17:50.460
and disaster, reversals in development gains,
increases in displacement, aggravated tensions

00:17:50.460 --> 00:17:56.390
over water and land, fragile states tipping
into chaos.

00:17:56.390 --> 00:18:01.850
Yet, greenhouse emissions are rising faster
than ever.

00:18:01.850 --> 00:18:06.760
Business as usual retains its hold.

00:18:06.760 --> 00:18:14.330
We must limit global temperature rise to two
degrees centigrade.

00:18:14.330 --> 00:18:21.309
We are far from there, and even that is enough
to cause dire consequences.

00:18:21.309 --> 00:18:29.140
If we continue along the current path, we
are close to a six degree increase.

00:18:29.140 --> 00:18:31.640
That will be a catastrophe.

00:18:31.640 --> 00:18:41.620
Too many leaders seem content to keep climate
change at arms' length and in a policy silo.

00:18:41.620 --> 00:18:48.970
To few grasp the need to bring the threat
to the center of global security, economic

00:18:48.970 --> 00:18:51.169
and financial management.

00:18:51.169 --> 00:19:00.549
It's time to move beyond spending enormous
sums addressing the damage and to make investments

00:19:00.549 --> 00:19:05.730
that will repay themselves many times over.

00:19:05.730 --> 00:19:13.010
Some countries are embracing the transition
to low-carbon, low-emissions future.

00:19:13.010 --> 00:19:19.520
They are adding electrical capacity through
renewable energy and avoiding the need to

00:19:19.520 --> 00:19:24.690
build new coal power plants.

00:19:24.690 --> 00:19:31.660
The renewable energy industry created 1.5
million new jobs last year.

00:19:31.660 --> 00:19:36.410
The cost of wind power continued to fall.

00:19:36.410 --> 00:19:44.169
With the right enabling public policies, close
to 80 percent of the world's energy supply

00:19:44.169 --> 00:19:49.500
could be met by renewables by mid-century.

00:19:49.500 --> 00:19:53.070
This is not utopian or science fiction.

00:19:53.070 --> 00:19:56.010
It is current fact.

00:19:56.010 --> 00:20:03.580
My sustainable energy for all initiative aims
to support and expand such a point.

00:20:03.580 --> 00:20:11.000
The green economy is an essential insurance
policy and investment in a safer future for

00:20:11.000 --> 00:20:12.000
all.

00:20:12.000 --> 00:20:20.110
A global climate change agreement would give
us the engine we need to advance us decisively

00:20:20.110 --> 00:20:21.780
on this path.

00:20:21.780 --> 00:20:29.840
I welcome President Obama's due resolve to
address climate change and give it high political

00:20:29.840 --> 00:20:32.500
priority.

00:20:32.500 --> 00:20:39.390
I'm reaching out to government and business
leaders to mobilize the capital and the political

00:20:39.390 --> 00:20:47.040
will for a global, legally binding climate
change treaty by 2015.

00:20:47.040 --> 00:20:56.820
World leaders have pledged to reach an agreement
and we must hold them to that promise.

00:20:56.820 --> 00:21:03.730
Ladies and gentlemen, before making my way
to Christiane Amanpour and your questions,

00:21:03.730 --> 00:21:12.429
let me violate my own rule, which I said only
two items -- mention two other issues of special

00:21:12.429 --> 00:21:13.429
urgency.

00:21:13.429 --> 00:21:17.110
First, the Israeli-Palestinian conflict.

00:21:17.110 --> 00:21:21.480
There's a change in the air across the Middle
East.

00:21:21.480 --> 00:21:25.990
It's long past time to resolve this conflict.

00:21:25.990 --> 00:21:30.760
We all know that a just and a permanent solution
looks like.

00:21:30.760 --> 00:21:39.930
In the years ahead, we must stop finding excuses
as to why the issues are too hard.

00:21:39.930 --> 00:21:43.990
Second, situations on the Korean peninsula.

00:21:43.990 --> 00:21:52.559
I have urged the new leadership of Pyongyang
to refrain from any further provocative actions,

00:21:52.559 --> 00:21:56.210
particularly another nuclear test.

00:21:56.210 --> 00:22:02.540
It is time for the DPRK authorities, who see
the unified call of the international community,

00:22:02.540 --> 00:22:09.110
to return to dialogue for the denuclearization
of the Korean peninsula.

00:22:09.110 --> 00:22:16.120
They should also address international concerns,
including about the dire humanitarian and

00:22:16.120 --> 00:22:18.730
human rights situations.

00:22:18.730 --> 00:22:26.610
Should they reverse course, both are open
for the DPRK to build a better future.

00:22:26.610 --> 00:22:32.700
As the secretary-general of the United Nations,
I stand ready to work with all concerned parties

00:22:32.700 --> 00:22:35.720
for peace and stability in the region.

00:22:35.720 --> 00:22:41.710
On the more general question of disarmament
-- here, too -- I welcome President Obama's

00:22:41.710 --> 00:22:47.250
heightened attention, including a push to
reduce nuclear arsenals.

00:22:47.250 --> 00:22:55.220
On my part, I continue to press for steps
to realize the goals of the five-point plan

00:22:55.220 --> 00:22:59.760
I set out early in my first term.

00:22:59.760 --> 00:23:08.080
Later this week, Wednesday and Thursday, I
will visit Washington, D.C. for talks on these

00:23:08.080 --> 00:23:09.770
and many other issues.

00:23:09.770 --> 00:23:16.570
I look forward to my discussions on Capitol
Hill and with the secretary of state John

00:23:16.570 --> 00:23:18.490
Kerry.

00:23:18.490 --> 00:23:25.900
My task as the secretary-general is to address
crises today while building solid foundations

00:23:25.900 --> 00:23:27.350
for tomorrow.

00:23:27.350 --> 00:23:31.200
I seek good international formulas.

00:23:31.200 --> 00:23:39.039
I try to make possible the sacrifices and
compromises that may be painful in the moment

00:23:39.039 --> 00:23:45.870
but which bring great dividends for coming
generations.

00:23:45.870 --> 00:23:53.470
We live in an age of monumental transitions
-- economic, demographic, political.

00:23:53.470 --> 00:23:59.110
Global interdependencies are deepening; transitional
threats are growing.

00:23:59.110 --> 00:24:05.910
This means we must make better use of the
United Nations machinery.

00:24:05.910 --> 00:24:11.480
From peacekeeping and peace- building, from
prevention to international criminal justice,

00:24:11.480 --> 00:24:20.059
our tools have proven more dynamic than most
people give us credit for.

00:24:20.059 --> 00:24:29.159
But we also recognize the need for our vehicle,
first created in the 1940s, to be equipped

00:24:29.159 --> 00:24:33.700
for the highways of the 21st century.

00:24:33.700 --> 00:24:37.809
International machinery does not operate on
its own.

00:24:37.809 --> 00:24:40.400
Hardware requires programmers.

00:24:40.400 --> 00:24:45.070
We need national leaders to think globally.

00:24:45.070 --> 00:24:48.980
We need a stronger sense of collective responsibility.

00:24:48.980 --> 00:24:56.080
And we need the United States, "our last best
hope," I quote.

00:24:56.080 --> 00:25:03.680
That is how President Kennedy, with help from
Ted Sorensen, famously described the United

00:25:03.680 --> 00:25:07.210
Nations in his inaugural address.

00:25:07.210 --> 00:25:11.620
He was speaking about a world in the grip
of an arms race.

00:25:11.620 --> 00:25:17.409
Now today we face a race against time.

00:25:17.409 --> 00:25:23.460
I look forward to the contributions that you
in this room will make towards helping us

00:25:23.460 --> 00:25:32.500
reach our shared destinations: a safer, more
just and more sustainable world for all.

00:25:32.500 --> 00:25:34.380
I thank you for your attention.

00:25:34.380 --> 00:25:35.710
Thank you.

00:25:35.710 --> 00:25:37.049
(Applause.)

00:25:37.049 --> 00:25:46.850
CHRISTIANE AMANPOUR (chief international correspondent,
CNN): So without further ado, because I know

00:25:46.850 --> 00:25:50.870
we're all on a schedule, I'm going to speak
to the secretary-general further for about

00:25:50.870 --> 00:25:54.590
10 minutes, and then we're going to open it
up to the members for questions.

00:25:54.590 --> 00:26:02.299
So, Mr. Secretary-General, when you've got
your papers sorted out, you've said that,

00:26:02.299 --> 00:26:08.130
you know, often you worry that the U.N. has
been marginalized and that people complain

00:26:08.130 --> 00:26:09.230
about that.

00:26:09.230 --> 00:26:14.460
You've spoken about how you don't measure
days and hours anymore when it comes to Syria,

00:26:14.460 --> 00:26:16.429
but you measure them in bodies.

00:26:16.429 --> 00:26:20.760
And you're talking about Syria being torn
limb from limb.

00:26:20.760 --> 00:26:24.320
So has the United Nations failed?

00:26:24.320 --> 00:26:27.940
Is this a failure of the international security
system?

00:26:27.940 --> 00:26:31.470
BAN: I don't think the United Nations has
failed.

00:26:31.470 --> 00:26:37.170
What I'm stressing is that the international
community must be united.

00:26:37.170 --> 00:26:40.970
As I said, division rules the day.

00:26:40.970 --> 00:26:48.100
Particularly, the Security Council members
should feel a collective responsibility for

00:26:48.100 --> 00:26:51.190
the humanity and for the history.

00:26:51.190 --> 00:26:57.289
How can they answer after many years after
this crisis is over?

00:26:57.289 --> 00:27:05.679
And whether they have done all what they should
have done, I as the secretary-general don't

00:27:05.679 --> 00:27:15.159
want to apologize to the international community
after many years for what I and we could have

00:27:15.159 --> 00:27:17.430
done for humanity.

00:27:17.430 --> 00:27:18.430
This is my message.

00:27:18.430 --> 00:27:26.380
For that -- first of all, Syrians, both parties,
they must stop killing each other, and they

00:27:26.380 --> 00:27:27.640
must sit together.

00:27:27.640 --> 00:27:34.650
Now there is a small window of opportunity
for both opposition forces and even government.

00:27:34.650 --> 00:27:39.460
AMANPOUR: Does your special representative,
the joint envoy Lakhdar Brahimi, believe that

00:27:39.460 --> 00:27:42.090
there's a real opportunity for talks right
now?

00:27:42.090 --> 00:27:46.799
You mentioned Mouaz al-Khatib, the opposition
leader, has offered to meet the members of

00:27:46.799 --> 00:27:51.530
the Assad regime, and in fact the Assad regime
has also said that it would like to hold talks

00:27:51.530 --> 00:27:55.029
as well, but there are lot of conditions attached
by both sides.

00:27:55.029 --> 00:27:59.059
Do you honestly believe that there is a chance
for talks?

00:27:59.059 --> 00:28:04.840
BAN: If there is a political will, commitment,
for humanity, there is a chance.

00:28:04.840 --> 00:28:10.640
Today I had a long talk with -- again, over
telephone -- with Lakhdar Brahimi.

00:28:10.640 --> 00:28:19.120
We discuss with him everything -- either myself,
my deputy secretary-general or Jeffrey Feltman,

00:28:19.120 --> 00:28:21.660
the head of the political department.

00:28:21.660 --> 00:28:23.690
We always discuss.

00:28:23.690 --> 00:28:33.030
And he met Moaz al-Khatib yesterday in Cairo,
and they discussed all this political transition.

00:28:33.030 --> 00:28:35.350
I think they are ready.

00:28:35.350 --> 00:28:42.400
And even if opposition forces are now ready
to talk with all the parties concerned, including

00:28:42.400 --> 00:28:54.799
even regime, for whom they rejected to meet,
and they asked us in the past that their condition

00:28:54.799 --> 00:28:58.020
was that President Assad should step down
before --

00:28:58.020 --> 00:28:59.610
AMANPOUR: Their condition now --

00:28:59.610 --> 00:29:00.610
BAN: Yes.

00:29:00.610 --> 00:29:03.659
AMANPOUR: Their condition now is to release
all the political prisoners.

00:29:03.659 --> 00:29:10.529
BAN: Yes, I have been urging Syrian authorities,
and I have conveyed my message to President

00:29:10.529 --> 00:29:19.720
Assad that he should do whatever he can to
create an atmosphere conducive for dialogue

00:29:19.720 --> 00:29:26.909
for political transition in releasing all
these prisoners -- and particularly women

00:29:26.909 --> 00:29:29.450
detained for political reasons.

00:29:29.450 --> 00:29:33.360
AMANPOUR: This has been going on for nearly
two years, and you've expressed frustration

00:29:33.360 --> 00:29:34.360
quite regularly.

00:29:34.360 --> 00:29:39.830
When I interviewed you last, I asked you what
is Plan B, and you said there is no Plan B.

00:29:39.830 --> 00:29:42.950
Now you've just talked about a collective
responsibility.

00:29:42.950 --> 00:29:48.429
Well, the United Nations adopted a responsibility
to protect.

00:29:48.429 --> 00:29:50.610
Has it failed in Syria?

00:29:50.610 --> 00:29:57.860
BAN: We have seen some good examples when
it comes to responsibility to protect.

00:29:57.860 --> 00:30:06.950
Now, this is a principle agreed upon and committed
by all the world's leaders in 2005, and the

00:30:06.950 --> 00:30:12.190
member states of the General Assembly have
been discussing this matter, how to operationalize

00:30:12.190 --> 00:30:14.820
this very important principle.

00:30:14.820 --> 00:30:19.890
We have seen good successes in case of Cote
d'Ivoire and Libya --

00:30:19.890 --> 00:30:21.330
AMANPOUR: Yeah, but in Syria.

00:30:21.330 --> 00:30:23.279
BAN: -- but Syria, it has not been applied.

00:30:23.279 --> 00:30:24.279
It has not --

00:30:24.279 --> 00:30:25.279
AMANPOUR: Well, right -

00:30:25.279 --> 00:30:26.279
BAN: It's been able to work.

00:30:26.279 --> 00:30:27.279
AMANPOUR: So has it failed?

00:30:27.279 --> 00:30:29.460
BAN: We are not working for failure.

00:30:29.460 --> 00:30:31.289
We are working for success.

00:30:31.289 --> 00:30:32.289
(Laughter.)

00:30:32.289 --> 00:30:35.710
AMANPOUR: I know you're working for success.

00:30:35.710 --> 00:30:37.440
Would you say it's a success, then?

00:30:37.440 --> 00:30:38.480
BAN: No.

00:30:38.480 --> 00:30:46.299
We have yet to work very hard -- (audio break)
-- we have to work very hard.

00:30:46.299 --> 00:30:53.590
That's why I'm asking world leaders, whether
they are in Syria or -- (audio break) -- region,

00:30:53.590 --> 00:30:56.020
Security Council -- they have to be united.

00:30:56.020 --> 00:31:04.149
They have to have a sense of responsibility
for humanity, for the future, for -- (audio

00:31:04.149 --> 00:31:05.149
break).

00:31:05.149 --> 00:31:12.309
AMANPOUR: As they were leaving the administration,
Secretary of Defense Leon Panetta, CIA Director

00:31:12.309 --> 00:31:18.149
-- former -- David Petraeus, outgoing Secretary
of State Hillary Clinton said that they all

00:31:18.149 --> 00:31:22.909
supported the idea of arming and training
the rebels.

00:31:22.909 --> 00:31:28.260
Do you think that diplomacy can work -- the
best efforts of Lakhdar Brahimi -- if it is

00:31:28.260 --> 00:31:33.450
not backed up by the use of force or the threat
of the use of force?

00:31:33.450 --> 00:31:37.669
In Syria I'm talking about.

00:31:37.669 --> 00:31:54.380
BAN: Basically at this point -- and I have
been urging both parties -- (audio break).

00:31:54.380 --> 00:32:00.240
AMANPOUR: Was it being recorded or it's just
not on the -- on the loudspeaker?

00:32:00.240 --> 00:32:01.980
(Audio break.)

00:32:01.980 --> 00:32:03.730
(Off-side conversation.)

00:32:03.730 --> 00:32:06.340
AMANPOUR: Live television.

00:32:06.340 --> 00:32:08.039
BAN: This is live?

00:32:08.039 --> 00:32:09.039
(Laughter.)

00:32:09.039 --> 00:32:10.110
AMANPOUR: It is.

00:32:10.110 --> 00:32:11.659
It's being streamed.

00:32:11.659 --> 00:32:15.320
It's being streamed, but don't worry.

00:32:15.320 --> 00:32:16.779
(Laughter.)

00:32:16.779 --> 00:32:26.640
OK, I will -- just to sort of -- (inaudible).

00:32:26.640 --> 00:32:34.100
So, in other words, as you -- as you seek
-- as you seek to try to have your envoys

00:32:34.100 --> 00:32:40.920
accomplish some diplomacy, would it be much
easier if it was backed up by the use of force?

00:32:40.920 --> 00:32:45.590
BAN: Basically, there is no military options.

00:32:45.590 --> 00:32:47.790
There is no military solution.

00:32:47.790 --> 00:32:57.480
Unfortunately, both sides seem to believe
determinedly that they can win over the other

00:32:57.480 --> 00:32:59.399
by military means.

00:32:59.399 --> 00:33:01.840
But that's wrong approach.

00:33:01.840 --> 00:33:08.900
All the pending issues should have been -- should
be resolved through a political dialogue.

00:33:08.900 --> 00:33:12.669
In the end, it's a political transition.

00:33:12.669 --> 00:33:22.520
I have been urging all the countries who may
be providing arms to both sides -- they should

00:33:22.520 --> 00:33:23.520
stop.

00:33:23.520 --> 00:33:28.170
Now, they -- both sides must stop the fighting
each other.

00:33:28.170 --> 00:33:34.180
AMANPOUR: You know, look, that would be great,
but it's just not going to happen until there's

00:33:34.180 --> 00:33:36.650
some kind of tipping point.

00:33:36.650 --> 00:33:43.610
And I was speaking to Senator McCain today,
who said that all the reasons for not intervening

00:33:43.610 --> 00:33:46.090
have now come true because of not intervening.

00:33:46.090 --> 00:33:53.321
In other words, the idea that one would create
a space for jihadis if one intervened, they're

00:33:53.321 --> 00:33:55.220
already there by not intervening.

00:33:55.220 --> 00:34:01.350
The idea that it would spill over into the
region by intervening has already happened

00:34:01.350 --> 00:34:04.720
by not intervening.

00:34:04.720 --> 00:34:09.869
So I know that you don't appreciate or want
the use of force, but how do you think this

00:34:09.869 --> 00:34:10.889
is going to stop?

00:34:10.889 --> 00:34:12.979
I mean, really?

00:34:12.979 --> 00:34:19.460
BAN: That's what Lakhdar Brahimi -- and we
are working very hard to talk with the parties

00:34:19.460 --> 00:34:27.079
concerned, Russia, United States and members
of the permanent -- permanent members of the

00:34:27.079 --> 00:34:28.079
Security Council.

00:34:28.079 --> 00:34:33.200
They should have a primary responsibility.

00:34:33.200 --> 00:34:38.940
And Lakhdar Brahimi has been meeting all the
parties in the region, and including Security

00:34:38.940 --> 00:34:40.339
Council members.

00:34:40.339 --> 00:34:47.469
Let diplomacy, let dialogue prevail over military
means.

00:34:47.469 --> 00:34:55.009
That's the best way and most practical way
to save for human lives.

00:34:55.009 --> 00:35:04.700
AMANPOUR: As you mentioned, there are some
4 million Syrians in need of help inside Syria.

00:35:04.700 --> 00:35:08.049
There's about three-quarters of a million
who have crossed the borders.

00:35:08.049 --> 00:35:12.479
One of your senior U.N. officials told me
the other day that it's very possible that

00:35:12.479 --> 00:35:15.959
some of the neighbors may slam the doors on
these refugees.

00:35:15.959 --> 00:35:17.549
They're drowning in refugees.

00:35:17.549 --> 00:35:19.349
It's affecting their own stability.

00:35:19.349 --> 00:35:21.489
They can't cope.

00:35:21.489 --> 00:35:25.519
Is that a worry, or do you think all this
money that you've gotten pledged in Kuwait

00:35:25.519 --> 00:35:26.559
is going to help?

00:35:26.559 --> 00:35:36.769
BAN: I'm extremely concerned about the current
situation when it comes to humanitarian situation.

00:35:36.769 --> 00:35:46.829
In the absence of a political solution, the
United Nations has been mobilizing all possible

00:35:46.829 --> 00:35:49.150
agencies and resources.

00:35:49.150 --> 00:35:56.600
That's why I decided to convene an international
high-level donors conference, where the member

00:35:56.600 --> 00:36:02.069
states have generously contributed more than
$1.5 billion.

00:36:02.069 --> 00:36:08.339
But this is for up to only June, end of June.

00:36:08.339 --> 00:36:13.259
The number of refugees are increasing day-by-day.

00:36:13.259 --> 00:36:20.450
Sometimes more than 3,000 people cross the
border overnight, through Jordan, Iraq, Lebanon

00:36:20.450 --> 00:36:22.069
and Turkey.

00:36:22.069 --> 00:36:27.170
It's almost impossible to handle all these
refugees.

00:36:27.170 --> 00:36:34.440
The total number is almost reaching 800,000
as of today, and there are 4 million people

00:36:34.440 --> 00:36:37.200
affected.

00:36:37.200 --> 00:36:40.339
And this is 2.5 million people internally
displaced.

00:36:40.339 --> 00:36:46.249
AMANPOUR: Do you worry that countries like
Jordan will just say enough, we can't have

00:36:46.249 --> 00:36:47.249
anymore?

00:36:47.249 --> 00:36:54.019
BAN: I visited the Zaatari camp in Jordan
and also camp -- a refugee camp in Turkey.

00:36:54.019 --> 00:37:02.549
My message has been consistent: Please keep
your border open.

00:37:02.549 --> 00:37:12.410
There were some cases that they were returning
these refugees, but I am urging them to keep

00:37:12.410 --> 00:37:15.289
this refugee flow.

00:37:15.289 --> 00:37:21.910
Whenever, wherever they want to find a place,
they should be accommodated.

00:37:21.910 --> 00:37:28.200
The United Nations will do our best efforts
to provide humanitarian assistance.

00:37:28.200 --> 00:37:31.209
AMANPOUR: Let's move on to North Korea.

00:37:31.209 --> 00:37:35.329
You said that you had urged the leaders of
North Korea not to conduct another nuclear

00:37:35.329 --> 00:37:36.619
test.

00:37:36.619 --> 00:37:40.050
Who have you spoken to and what has the response
been?

00:37:40.050 --> 00:37:49.119
BAN: Most of all I've been using my public
statement as the secretary-general of the

00:37:49.119 --> 00:37:58.170
United Nations, and the messages have been
conveyed to Pyongyang through the major countries

00:37:58.170 --> 00:37:59.549
in the -- in the region.

00:37:59.549 --> 00:38:01.299
AMANPOUR: So you haven't picked up the phone
and talked --

00:38:01.299 --> 00:38:07.829
BAN: I have not been able to talk directly
to leadership in Pyongyang.

00:38:07.829 --> 00:38:10.190
They don't work in that way.

00:38:10.190 --> 00:38:11.190
(Laughter.)

00:38:11.190 --> 00:38:19.890
I don't think whether anybody has ever spoken
over the telephone to North Korean leadership.

00:38:19.890 --> 00:38:21.339
That's what they do.

00:38:21.339 --> 00:38:23.539
So sometimes it's very, very difficult.

00:38:23.539 --> 00:38:28.369
AMANPOUR: So what will the new president of
South Korea -- what will the new president

00:38:28.369 --> 00:38:30.460
of South Korea be able to do?

00:38:30.460 --> 00:38:35.069
She's already spoken about wanting to change,
to an extent, the policy of her predecessor.

00:38:35.069 --> 00:38:44.849
BAN: She has a very good practical vision
in this Korean Peninsula confidence and trust

00:38:44.849 --> 00:38:45.849
process.

00:38:45.849 --> 00:38:53.460
She met former leader Kim Jong Il in 2002.

00:38:53.460 --> 00:39:00.529
And I think that she's in a better position
to really break this deadlocked situation

00:39:00.529 --> 00:39:01.849
on the Korean Peninsula.

00:39:01.849 --> 00:39:11.869
Now, my mission has been that if DPRK conducts
nuclear tests at this time, unfortunately

00:39:11.869 --> 00:39:18.160
it may effectively tie the hands of the new
president of Korea, that it may take a long

00:39:18.160 --> 00:39:26.670
time before anybody can really be able to
think about the -- normalizing the relationship.

00:39:26.670 --> 00:39:41.650
Therefore, I would urge again the DPRK authorities
to focus their priority on making their people

00:39:41.650 --> 00:39:48.209
economically sustainable and developing of
people is more important at this time.

00:39:48.209 --> 00:39:55.839
If they run the nuclear test, this not only
runs counter and violates the relevant Security

00:39:55.839 --> 00:40:00.890
Council resolutions, but it's going toward
the wrong direction -- wrong direction.

00:40:00.890 --> 00:40:02.589
AMANPOUR: You mentioned Mali.

00:40:02.589 --> 00:40:08.030
The U.N. and the Security Council are in talks,
I think, about a potential peacekeeping force

00:40:08.030 --> 00:40:09.569
for Mali.

00:40:09.569 --> 00:40:11.619
Is that something that you can see happening?

00:40:11.619 --> 00:40:13.599
Are the talks far along?

00:40:13.599 --> 00:40:15.809
Will there be a U.N. peacekeeping force there?

00:40:15.809 --> 00:40:21.989
BAN: I know that members of the Security Council
are discussing this matter.

00:40:21.989 --> 00:40:25.539
And in fact, you know, I had some initial
discussions with the members of the Security

00:40:25.539 --> 00:40:26.890
Council on this matter.

00:40:26.890 --> 00:40:32.949
But the situation at this time is still very
precarious, very dangerous at this time.

00:40:32.949 --> 00:40:40.339
But we need to assess the situation on the
ground before we will be able to discuss establishing

00:40:40.339 --> 00:40:42.259
the U.N. peacekeeping operation.

00:40:42.259 --> 00:40:43.809
AMANPOUR: Do you think it should happen?

00:40:43.809 --> 00:40:50.390
BAN: I think it's a matter of time when the
-- these coalition forces will be able to

00:40:50.390 --> 00:40:56.299
address the security concerns on the ground,
then we will be able to discuss this matter.

00:40:56.299 --> 00:41:05.170
AMANPOUR: On a different issue in a different
country, you had a lot of U.N. and NGO humanitarian

00:41:05.170 --> 00:41:09.660
medical workers trying to vaccinate populations
in Pakistan.

00:41:09.660 --> 00:41:10.980
And they are being killed.

00:41:10.980 --> 00:41:12.960
I mean, they are just being killed.

00:41:12.960 --> 00:41:16.710
And some of these vaccination programs are
having to be suspended.

00:41:16.710 --> 00:41:20.359
I mean, do you see any way out of that?

00:41:20.359 --> 00:41:21.359
What is going on?

00:41:21.359 --> 00:41:24.789
And have you had any conversations that lead
you to believe that that's something that's

00:41:24.789 --> 00:41:26.779
going to stop or be reversed?

00:41:26.779 --> 00:41:35.599
BAN: It is totally unacceptable that health
workers are being killed while vaccinating

00:41:35.599 --> 00:41:40.190
polio in Pakistan, in Nigeria.

00:41:40.190 --> 00:41:46.390
Unfortunately, again yesterday three more
Korean health workers, doctors, were killed

00:41:46.390 --> 00:41:49.069
by these people.

00:41:49.069 --> 00:41:52.869
And I have condemned them strong -- in the
strongest possible terms.

00:41:52.869 --> 00:42:01.339
They are working selflessly for benefit of
people, for health of those young people to

00:42:01.339 --> 00:42:03.019
eradicate the polio.

00:42:03.019 --> 00:42:11.499
There are only three countries in the world
at this time where polio still exists -- that's

00:42:11.499 --> 00:42:14.829
Afghanistan, Pakistan and Nigeria.

00:42:14.829 --> 00:42:21.130
We are working very hard with the leaders
of those countries to eradicate once and for

00:42:21.130 --> 00:42:23.079
all this polio.

00:42:23.079 --> 00:42:27.539
AMANPOUR: If the violence doesn't stop, will
these programs be ended?

00:42:27.539 --> 00:42:36.579
BAN: I think we will have to continue vaccinating
these people to save their children's lives.

00:42:36.579 --> 00:42:39.410
Otherwise, they will be dying needlessly.

00:42:39.410 --> 00:42:42.699
AMANPOUR: Secretary General, thank you very
much.

00:42:42.699 --> 00:42:43.789
BAN: Thank you very much.

00:42:43.789 --> 00:42:49.910
AMANPOUR: And now we're going to open it to
questions.

00:42:49.910 --> 00:42:51.700
(Applause.)

00:42:51.700 --> 00:42:54.970
So I have a couple of instructions here regarding
how to do the questions.

00:42:54.970 --> 00:42:57.779
I think -- are there microphones?

00:42:57.779 --> 00:42:58.779
Yes.

00:42:58.779 --> 00:42:59.779
OK.

00:42:59.779 --> 00:43:01.969
So I saw a hand over there first.

00:43:01.969 --> 00:43:05.890
Right there, this gentleman.

00:43:05.890 --> 00:43:07.349
Yeah, you got the mic there?

00:43:07.349 --> 00:43:08.349
QUESTIONER: Thank you.

00:43:08.349 --> 00:43:10.779
Dan Altman from NYU and North Yard Economics.

00:43:10.779 --> 00:43:15.009
Mr. Secretary General, I'd like to ask you
about an area where you do have some real

00:43:15.009 --> 00:43:20.699
leverage and not just moral suasion, which
is the running of the U.N. itself.

00:43:20.699 --> 00:43:26.199
The U.N., despite its effectiveness in some
areas, which you mentioned, still has a reputation

00:43:26.199 --> 00:43:33.140
for opaque administration, for hiring and
coddling perhaps incompetent workers and also

00:43:33.140 --> 00:43:37.059
for perhaps wasting some money here and there,
though many governments and companies do that

00:43:37.059 --> 00:43:38.809
as well.

00:43:38.809 --> 00:43:44.759
And I would like to ask you: What can you
to do improve the transparency and accountability

00:43:44.759 --> 00:43:49.450
of your organization, since those themes are
two things that you emphasize so much?

00:43:49.450 --> 00:43:52.940
I can tell you, from my own experience, I've
been unable to figure how much, for example,

00:43:52.940 --> 00:43:53.940
the --

00:43:53.940 --> 00:43:54.940
AMANPOUR: Not too long, OK?

00:43:54.940 --> 00:43:55.940
Not a speech.

00:43:55.940 --> 00:43:56.940
QUESTIONER: There's a last sentence --

00:43:56.940 --> 00:43:57.940
AMANPOUR: Not a speech.

00:43:57.940 --> 00:43:58.940
And please, everybody, members, not a speech.

00:43:58.940 --> 00:43:59.940
Otherwise, we don't get anywhere.

00:43:59.940 --> 00:44:02.180
QUESTIONER: How much, for example, did the
Rio Plus 20 conference cost the U.N. as a

00:44:02.180 --> 00:44:03.180
whole?

00:44:03.180 --> 00:44:04.180
AMANPOUR: OK.

00:44:04.180 --> 00:44:05.180
Thank you.

00:44:05.180 --> 00:44:06.180
QUESTIONER: Maybe that's a good place to start.

00:44:06.180 --> 00:44:07.180
AMANPOUR: Thank you.

00:44:07.180 --> 00:44:13.699
BAN: First of all, improving efficiency by
enhancing transparency and accountability

00:44:13.699 --> 00:44:21.160
has been one of my top priorities as a change
in management policy.

00:44:21.160 --> 00:44:23.920
I think we have achieved a lot.

00:44:23.920 --> 00:44:27.760
The United Nations is different from the past.

00:44:27.760 --> 00:44:35.079
Of course in the past the United Nations must
have been working very hard to improve their

00:44:35.079 --> 00:44:36.719
way of working.

00:44:36.719 --> 00:44:45.579
But since I took over this responsibility,
from day one, the accountability, ethics,

00:44:45.579 --> 00:44:52.999
high level of ethics, and transparency and
openness and accessibility have been some

00:44:52.999 --> 00:44:57.910
keywords for my organization, and I'm going
to continue to do that.

00:44:57.910 --> 00:45:02.069
You talk about the Rio Plus 20 summit meeting.

00:45:02.069 --> 00:45:04.720
Again, we have achieved a lot.

00:45:04.720 --> 00:45:14.410
For just one example, we had for the first
time to use this smart conference, the paperless

00:45:14.410 --> 00:45:15.410
conference.

00:45:15.410 --> 00:45:25.920
We used -- (off mic) -- some tens of thousands
-- like 30,000 pages are printed and distributed

00:45:25.920 --> 00:45:26.940
to the member states.

00:45:26.940 --> 00:45:30.349
Otherwise, it might have been millions of
pages.

00:45:30.349 --> 00:45:38.720
In the recent Doha climate change conference
in December last year, we also reduced a lot

00:45:38.720 --> 00:45:45.489
-- millions of pages of paper by using smart
paperless conference.

00:45:45.489 --> 00:45:51.170
And this is what -- the United Nations is
trying to improve the way we work.

00:45:51.170 --> 00:45:53.859
AMANPOUR: Over there.

00:45:53.859 --> 00:45:54.859
Ken.

00:45:54.859 --> 00:45:59.749
Ken Roth of Human Rights Watch.

00:45:59.749 --> 00:46:01.670
And I know you know how to ask a question.

00:46:01.670 --> 00:46:02.670
(Laughter.)

00:46:02.670 --> 00:46:05.539
QUESTIONER: Mr. Secretary-General, I would
like to ask you about a particularly courageous

00:46:05.539 --> 00:46:10.440
step you took in your first term in commissioning
the so-called Petrie report, which looked

00:46:10.440 --> 00:46:14.690
very critically at the U.N.'s operations in
Sri Lanka in the waning months of the war

00:46:14.690 --> 00:46:20.869
with Tamil Tigers and essentially criticized
the U.N. for prioritizing humanitarian presence

00:46:20.869 --> 00:46:25.009
over speaking out over the indiscriminate
shelling and murder of 40,000.

00:46:25.009 --> 00:46:29.380
There are two recommendations it makes that
I want to ask you for a progress report on.

00:46:29.380 --> 00:46:36.160
One was the recommendation that a senior political
person be put in charge of any crisis like

00:46:36.160 --> 00:46:42.519
that, so that the U.N.'s priorities were properly
reflected, and second, that there be a rapid

00:46:42.519 --> 00:46:47.699
deployment of human rights monitors, to make
it contemporary, in a situation like Mali

00:46:47.699 --> 00:46:48.699
or Syria.

00:46:48.699 --> 00:46:50.170
Where does that stand?

00:46:50.170 --> 00:46:57.789
BAN: Now I have decided, when it comes to
Mali, we didn't have any mission there.

00:46:57.789 --> 00:47:06.549
With -- soon as this crisis began, within
one week I had dispatched and deployed on

00:47:06.549 --> 00:47:10.700
the ground this high political mission.

00:47:10.700 --> 00:47:18.229
And I deployed, in close consultation with
the high commissioner for human rights, more

00:47:18.229 --> 00:47:20.269
humanitarian monitors on the ground.

00:47:20.269 --> 00:47:22.589
We will try to increase more.

00:47:22.589 --> 00:47:32.790
The -- protecting human rights and also humanitarian
assistance -- this is a top priority in all

00:47:32.790 --> 00:47:38.310
the conflict zones, as we have been doing
in Sri Lanka and elsewhere.

00:47:38.310 --> 00:47:44.130
And in that regard, I appreciate Mr. Ken Roth,
with whom I have been working very closely.

00:47:44.130 --> 00:47:47.460
Your advice has been quite appreciated.

00:47:47.460 --> 00:47:49.900
Thank you very much.

00:47:49.900 --> 00:47:50.900
Thank --

00:47:50.900 --> 00:47:52.329
AMANPOUR: Right here.

00:47:52.329 --> 00:47:55.369
Can we get a microphone?

00:47:55.369 --> 00:47:57.200
QUESTIONER: Felice Gaer.

00:47:57.200 --> 00:48:02.390
Mr. Secretary-General, when you spoke here
in 2006, before you were elected, you said

00:48:02.390 --> 00:48:06.430
that the responsibility to protect was one
of the issues that the -- I -- a top issue

00:48:06.430 --> 00:48:09.630
for you, needed to be substantiated.

00:48:09.630 --> 00:48:14.489
There is no one currently in the position
of special representative on responsibility

00:48:14.489 --> 00:48:15.489
to protect.

00:48:15.489 --> 00:48:18.099
Do you intend to fill that position?

00:48:18.099 --> 00:48:23.199
BAN: I was here in May 2006.

00:48:23.199 --> 00:48:28.369
In fact, at that time I was one of the candidates
for this job.

00:48:28.369 --> 00:48:33.499
I'm lucky, you know, fortunate to be chosen
as the secretary-general to serve the United

00:48:33.499 --> 00:48:34.499
Nations.

00:48:34.499 --> 00:48:42.880
Today, this afternoon, I read -- I just particularly
went through what I said the last time, in

00:48:42.880 --> 00:48:43.880
2006.

00:48:43.880 --> 00:48:44.880
(Laughter.)

00:48:44.880 --> 00:48:48.329
So you pointed out exactly what I said.

00:48:48.329 --> 00:48:56.690
One of my campaign pledges at that time was
that if and when I would be elected the secretary-general,

00:48:56.690 --> 00:49:04.680
I would do my best to operationalize the concept
of responsibility to protect.

00:49:04.680 --> 00:49:08.180
I will continue to do that.

00:49:08.180 --> 00:49:16.770
As you know, I appointed the special adviser
on responsibility to protect.

00:49:16.770 --> 00:49:26.229
It has been quite difficult, how to give it
this level of comfort of member states on

00:49:26.229 --> 00:49:27.680
this principle.

00:49:27.680 --> 00:49:31.449
It has been quite a sensitive and patient
process.

00:49:31.449 --> 00:49:37.739
But we have had the several rounds of General
Assembly discussions, and there was a consensus

00:49:37.739 --> 00:49:39.069
resolutions.

00:49:39.069 --> 00:49:47.019
We are moving, even though it's not moving
fast enough as we have been expecting, as

00:49:47.019 --> 00:49:51.490
you may be expecting -- I think we are going
toward the right direction.

00:49:51.490 --> 00:50:01.109
And at this time Mr. Deng, who worked as the
registrar of ICTR, International Criminal

00:50:01.109 --> 00:50:09.769
Tribunal for Rwanda, is now working as a special
adviser on prevention of genocide and special

00:50:09.769 --> 00:50:13.789
adviser for responsibility to protect.

00:50:13.789 --> 00:50:18.299
But when the time comes, you know, I would
try to point -- separate the special adviser

00:50:18.299 --> 00:50:20.470
on that.

00:50:20.470 --> 00:50:24.720
Thank you.

00:50:24.720 --> 00:50:28.099
QUESTIONER: Evelyn Leopold (sp).

00:50:28.099 --> 00:50:30.279
You say you're going to Washington.

00:50:30.279 --> 00:50:34.479
We happen to have a Congress that wants to
cut the budget.

00:50:34.479 --> 00:50:39.769
The Republicans are not that happy with the
U.N. They will reflect some of the same criticism

00:50:39.769 --> 00:50:41.849
that the first questioner had.

00:50:41.849 --> 00:50:45.789
Whether it's true or not, they don't very
much like foreigners.

00:50:45.789 --> 00:50:52.160
Aside from your -- aside from your trip, what
are you doing to promote the United Nations

00:50:52.160 --> 00:50:53.829
in the United States?

00:50:53.829 --> 00:51:03.579
BAN: I have been visiting Washington, D.C.,
and visiting and meeting the members of Congress,

00:51:03.579 --> 00:51:06.640
both House of Representatives and Senate.

00:51:06.640 --> 00:51:15.420
I'm going to have meetings with both the Foreign
Relations Committee in the Senate and in the

00:51:15.420 --> 00:51:17.239
House of Representatives.

00:51:17.239 --> 00:51:27.289
I believe that United Nations and United States
share same goals and objectives.

00:51:27.289 --> 00:51:34.640
When U.N. was created, U.S. played a crucially
important role, in fact.

00:51:34.640 --> 00:51:38.670
Therefore, we need the support of the United
States.

00:51:38.670 --> 00:51:48.009
Twenty- two percent to 27 percent of U.N.
budget is being borne by the United States,

00:51:48.009 --> 00:51:51.269
the largest contributor.

00:51:51.269 --> 00:51:59.709
And on top of this assessed contribution,
U.S. pays a lot of extrabudgetary contributions

00:51:59.709 --> 00:52:04.589
on development and human rights and security
and peace issues.

00:52:04.589 --> 00:52:06.459
We are very much grateful.

00:52:06.459 --> 00:52:12.359
That's why it's crucially important for any
secretary-general of the United Nations or

00:52:12.359 --> 00:52:17.969
any staff of the United Nations to have strong
support of the United Nations -- United States.

00:52:17.969 --> 00:52:25.439
That is why I always try to meet, starting
from president and secretary of state and

00:52:25.439 --> 00:52:27.799
members of the Congress.

00:52:27.799 --> 00:52:37.720
And I also want to get support from the citizens
of the United States.

00:52:37.720 --> 00:52:42.000
That's why I've been traveling a lot around
the United States.

00:52:42.000 --> 00:52:50.410
United Nations Association of U.N. and also
United States -- United Nations Association

00:52:50.410 --> 00:52:56.030
of United States and also U.N. Foundation
-- they have been playing a great role to

00:52:56.030 --> 00:52:57.119
help United Nations.

00:52:57.119 --> 00:53:00.780
AMANPOUR: So before I take another one from
the floor, I need to ask you a question from

00:53:00.780 --> 00:53:01.780
a national member.

00:53:01.780 --> 00:53:03.369
It came over via email.

00:53:03.369 --> 00:53:06.559
And this is from Nancy Aossey, International
Medical Corps.

00:53:06.559 --> 00:53:08.519
She's in Santa Monica, California.

00:53:08.519 --> 00:53:13.019
She wants to know, how do you envision the
U.N. better leveraging the impact of NGOs

00:53:13.019 --> 00:53:18.059
to support the 4 million Syrians who we've
been talking about in need of help?

00:53:18.059 --> 00:53:20.989
How are you planning to make that happen?

00:53:20.989 --> 00:53:24.949
And what critical needs, she asked, are the
U.N. prioritizing?

00:53:24.949 --> 00:53:34.049
BAN: Working together with the NGOs is crucially
important.

00:53:34.049 --> 00:53:39.509
In case of Syria, we have very limited access.

00:53:39.509 --> 00:53:48.559
We normally work together with Red Crescent
of Syria and International Red Cross Society,

00:53:48.559 --> 00:53:55.109
ICRC, and International Red Cross Federation
-- Federation of Red Cross.

00:53:55.109 --> 00:54:05.029
And many of the humanitarian-related agencies
and, you know, organizations, they provide

00:54:05.029 --> 00:54:07.569
the generous -- (inaudible) -- generous resources.

00:54:07.569 --> 00:54:11.279
They provide a lot of access.

00:54:11.279 --> 00:54:12.489
They play very important role.

00:54:12.489 --> 00:54:16.930
AMANPOUR: There are a lot of people in Syria
who are complaining now that they're not getting

00:54:16.930 --> 00:54:21.119
the stuff that they need; they're cold, they're
hungry, they don't have enough medicine, despite

00:54:21.119 --> 00:54:22.489
your best efforts.

00:54:22.489 --> 00:54:23.650
What are you prioritizing?

00:54:23.650 --> 00:54:27.969
BAN: There is a limit for the United Nations
at this time.

00:54:27.969 --> 00:54:38.519
First of all, access program and the resources,
the -- OCHA has been organizing this one -- it's

00:54:38.519 --> 00:54:42.959
a humanitarian coordinator -- together with
World Food Program.

00:54:42.959 --> 00:54:50.969
United Nations High Commission for Refugees
and WHO and all other -- UNICEF, all other

00:54:50.969 --> 00:54:56.219
humanitarian- related agencies are working
very, very hard in close consultation.

00:54:56.219 --> 00:55:07.069
I myself chaired -- chairing -- (inaudible)
-- quite often to have coordinated and structured

00:55:07.069 --> 00:55:12.239
way of delivering assistance.

00:55:12.239 --> 00:55:17.019
QUESTIONER: (Inaudible.)

00:55:17.019 --> 00:55:21.929
You have the tools to go to the Security Council
when it is a difficult situation, as it is

00:55:21.929 --> 00:55:23.589
right now on Syria, (and it stopped).

00:55:23.589 --> 00:55:29.319
You as secretary-general can actually convene
the council on a matter so grave, or the membership

00:55:29.319 --> 00:55:34.359
of the General Assembly can go -- (inaudible)
-- for peace to the G-8 or General Assembly

00:55:34.359 --> 00:55:35.970
and do something about it.

00:55:35.970 --> 00:55:36.970
Is it time?

00:55:36.970 --> 00:55:40.400
When will it be time for Syria to do that?

00:55:40.400 --> 00:55:45.589
And why do you keep saying that there is no
need for Jeffersonian or Swiss-like democracy

00:55:45.589 --> 00:55:46.609
in the Arab region.

00:55:46.609 --> 00:55:49.719
In that way, aren't you just endorsing a mitigated
democracy?

00:55:49.719 --> 00:55:50.829
Why not?

00:55:50.829 --> 00:55:55.980
Why not demand that when they promised that
by election?

00:55:55.980 --> 00:56:02.980
BAN: Primary responsibility is with -- rests
with the Security Council.

00:56:02.980 --> 00:56:14.150
At the same time, General Assembly also held
several sessions to discuss the Syrian situation.

00:56:14.150 --> 00:56:24.900
However, I believe that the Security Council
is fully aware of the importance and urgency

00:56:24.900 --> 00:56:31.229
and seriousness of this issue, therefore I'm
urging always the Security Council should

00:56:31.229 --> 00:56:35.559
take a collective and urgent responsibility
on this matter.

00:56:35.559 --> 00:56:37.910
AMANPOUR: We have time for one last question.

00:56:37.910 --> 00:56:42.150
You didn't -- you didn't finish your question
about why you don't believe they have the

00:56:42.150 --> 00:56:44.710
right to Jeffersonian democracy.

00:56:44.710 --> 00:56:52.039
BAN: Jeffersonian democracy is an ideal democracy,
most ideal.

00:56:52.039 --> 00:57:02.499
But the Syrian people at this time, first
and foremost, they have to stop this fighting,

00:57:02.499 --> 00:57:04.400
military fighting again.

00:57:04.400 --> 00:57:05.670
AMANPOUR: One last question.

00:57:05.670 --> 00:57:08.489
Yes, sir, your hand is up.

00:57:08.489 --> 00:57:14.039
QUESTIONER: Michael Doyle, the chairman of
your U.N. Democracy Fund advisory board, from

00:57:14.039 --> 00:57:15.039
Columbia University.

00:57:15.039 --> 00:57:16.039
(Laughter.)

00:57:16.039 --> 00:57:17.459
I have a question for you, Secretary-General.

00:57:17.459 --> 00:57:24.400
Many people have come to the view that the
R2P route through the Security Council has

00:57:24.400 --> 00:57:28.969
failed because of the stalemate in the council,
and therefore their suggestion, since it's

00:57:28.969 --> 00:57:33.699
already been raised here, of a Plan B, which
is arming the rebels -- could you tell us

00:57:33.699 --> 00:57:42.259
your concern about Plan B, arming the rebels
as a way to resolve the civil war in Syria?

00:57:42.259 --> 00:57:52.109
BAN: I think from the beginning, this -- the
crisis began because of -- (inaudible) -- that

00:57:52.109 --> 00:58:02.249
either side seem to have thought that they
would win and solve this problem through military

00:58:02.249 --> 00:58:03.249
means.

00:58:03.249 --> 00:58:08.140
The first began -- it began with the Syrians'
government.

00:58:08.140 --> 00:58:16.699
When people were demonstrating, expressing
their genuine aspirations through free expression,

00:58:16.699 --> 00:58:24.289
free will, then government repressed and responded
by killing people.

00:58:24.289 --> 00:58:35.329
That both is counter -- (inaudible) -- response
by the opposition forces through violent means.

00:58:35.329 --> 00:58:44.170
Therefore, I believe that continuing this
way, you cannot find any solution of this

00:58:44.170 --> 00:58:45.170
Syrian crisis.

00:58:45.170 --> 00:58:52.709
Therefore, without any condition, they must
stop this fighting and engage in dialogue.

00:58:52.709 --> 00:58:55.829
That's the only viable solution at this time.

00:58:55.829 --> 00:59:05.619
Just arming either side, opposition forces
and Syrian, by the country's concern, this

00:59:05.619 --> 00:59:09.469
will only deteriorate the situation.

00:59:09.469 --> 00:59:11.119
(Exhales.)

00:59:11.119 --> 00:59:12.769
(Laughter.)

00:59:12.769 --> 00:59:15.099
AMANPOUR: Thank you very much.

00:59:15.099 --> 00:59:19.049
I mean, it's hard to know how it can get any
worse, but it can because it can go on and

00:59:19.049 --> 00:59:20.049
on and on.

00:59:20.049 --> 00:59:23.039
But let's hope that some sanity prevails at
some point.

00:59:23.039 --> 00:59:26.079
Secretary-General, thank you very much indeed
for joining us and members of the Council

00:59:26.079 --> 00:59:27.079
on Foreign Relations.

00:59:27.079 --> 00:59:27.080
(Applause.)

